UID:10052531-1792252800-1792263600@industrycity.com
SUMMARY:Sloppy Heads\, Chris Forsyth's What is Now\, Wet Tuna
DESCRIPTION:Industry City and Get It Right Music in association with Murmrr Presents are happy to present a stacked triple bill including Sloppy Heads\, Chris Forsyth’s What is Now\, and Wet Tuna! \nEvent Info\nDate: October 17th\, 2026\nDoors: 3:00pm\nShow: 4:00-7:00pm\nTickets: $15 per person plus fees\nThe first 50 people to buy tickets get a free 12-ounce beer\, well drink\, 6 ounce wine or N/A beverage. \nAddress: Industry City Bandshell Stage\, Courtyard 1/2 at Industry City in Brooklyn New York between 36th and 37th street and 2nd and 3rd Avenues.\nENTER 238 36th Street\, Brooklyn\, NY on your GPS device \nThe venue is located in an open-air courtyard featuring a full concert sound stage. Fans can get affordable drinks from the venue’s bar and order food from over 17 options inside the adjoining food hall. Concert is rain or shine. \nHow to get here:\nTake the D\, N\, R lines to 36th street station in Brooklyn and walk west one block to Industry City. 3 stops from Manhattan. 30 minutes from Herald Square.\nFrom Long Island\, take the LIRR to Atlantic Terminal then take the D\, N or R train to 36th street. \nMore about the bands:\nTHE SLOPPY HEADS!\nThrill to the electrifying guitar wizardry of Jimmy Jumpjump!\nGasp at the audacity of the legendary Ariella Stok aka Starmaiden to whom shame is but a word in the dictionary!\nGrope to the exotic jungle rhythms of Bill the Drummer!\nIn one of their sought after East Coast appearances since the release of their second album\, Sometimes Just One Second on Shrimper Records.\nBop on down to see them perform live in concert\, where anything can happen and usually does. Fun for kids from 2 to 82. \nChris Forsyth’s WHAT IS NOW is a new collaborative project from Philadelphia guitarist Chris Forsyth (BASIC\, Solar Motel Band) featuring double bassist John Moran (Bark Culture\, Daniel Villareal Trio) and drummer Joey Sullivan (Florry\, Bark Culture).\nTogether they create spontaneous song forms exploring lyrically ambient moods\, repetition\, rhythmic interplay\, and a mindful leaderless-ness. As suggested by the players’ pedigrees\, elements or motorik propulsion\, trance jazz\, and deconstructed rock moves abound.\nThe Bandcamp- and merch table-only CD “Both / And” is the debut release\, about which Laura Snapes wrote this in the Guardian: “The Philadelphia guitarist swaps his usual Television-indebted questing riffage for 25 minutes of tingly\, exploratory improv alongside double bassist John Moran and drummer Joey Sullivan: imagine a scruffier Necks.” \nWET TUNA was formed for all beings. Legendary DJ/HEAD Jeff Conklin says “revel in the earth/space magic!” Their new record is called “VAST”. Matt “MV” Valentine with Jim Bliss & Erika Elder are the triad. They specialize in homegrown dubwise song exploration.\nThe kaleidoscopic sonic agriculture of Wet Tuna has long inhabited its own universe. Over the past five years\, they have established themselves as one of the most forward thinking and anarchic of the many groups rethinking and reconceptualizing the notion of the “jam band\,” all the while frustrating critical attempts to slot the group’s music into any scene\, sound\, or easily classifiable genre fad.\nThere remains a beautiful simplicity at the core of MV’s workbench alchemy; as with the music of Can\, there is in his productions almost always a lodestar fixed within the eye of whatever maelstrom or fragmented primal abstractions surround it. On Warping All By Yourself\, funk and soul are both the unifying principles and the unlikely bedrocks upon which everything else orbits and rests\, resulting in a concoction of iconoclastic\, free-dancing music that has as much in common with Funkadelic as it does Crazy Horse.
URL:https://industrycity.com/event/sloppy-heads-chris-forsyths-what-is-now-wet-tuna/
CATEGORIES:Live Events
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://industrycity.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Screen-Shot-2026-09-21-at-6.03.40-PM.png
END:VEVENT
